ards this  Summit as particularly important  all the decisions about these trips are  always extremely symbolic he could have  made this a virtual Summit If he if he'd  wanted to  um instead it means he can do a kind of  comfort zone multilateralism almost  ahead of the party Congress it gives him  a chance to see some of China's closest  Partners especially Putin of course with  whom really these meetings have always  been a priority since the very beginning  of his time as party secretary and it  means he can put on this strong show of  a non-western gathering Counterpoint to  G7 to Nato at an organization the  Shanghai cooperation organization that's  of China's own creation I think she at  the moment wants to show that in this  struggle with the United States in the  struggle with the West that China has  friends too and then it can put on a  kind of visible show of that  all right I'm I'm going to have that  expression put on a mug I think comfort  zone multilateralism  um in February before the the Russian  invasion of Ukraine uh the two leaders  declared there were no limits to their  partnership China also as you said  hasn't condemned Russia's Invasion so  is beijing's support for Moscow  unconditional  well political support yes diplomatic  propaganda as we've seen and as you  mentioned in the segment some forms of  economic support too the natural  resource purchases but but also helping  Russia navigate some of the financial  constraints that the U.S sanctions have  imposed and I think there are other  areas there's forms of military support  that China will be wary or about  um even certain arms supplies uh this  isn't really material to the conflict  and the reforms of sanctioned support as  well where I think China certainly  doesn't want to impose costs on its own  companies where it's still quite  dependent on Western technology and the  Western Financial system but it's  certainly unconditional support in the  sense that it doesn't really matter what  Russia does China doesn't react to  stories of Russian atrocities or as you  mentioned in in the story attacks on  civilian infrastructure there's just  indifference to the actual conduct of  the war and I think they want to show  that they will continue to give  solidarity even if as we see at the  moment the Russian campaign is failing  so in that sense I think it that there  are significant the unconditional  elements to the forms of support that  China provides right and just talking  about this this this idea that the  Russian effort war effort is failing  they have the two countries have  conducted joint military exercises this  year is Russia's apparent failure in  Ukraine causing China perhaps to rethink  their military relationship  well I mean China had always been  with the Russian military operations  particularly in Crimea and in Syria and  they sort of hoped and expected that  Moscow would be able to pull off  something I think similar this time so  they were surprised that this was such a  failure they'll have been conducting  their assessments on on what went wrong  I think the sense had been that Russia  is good at the kind of lightning type  operations and less good at the longer  attritional ones like I think that will  certainly be be reinforced and but I  think the biggest concern on the Chinese  side with this will be what it might do  to Russia's Global capabilities that no  limits agreement in February that you  mentioned was was very much about the  two sides working together in third  regions in including militarily and  we've seen that to a certain extent even  in recent months in Asia but also the  Middle East in Africa I think the  concern China will have is whether  Russia's capabilities have been and  still are being worn down by this war  and therefore what level of cooperation  is likely to be able to sustain as a  result of that but I don't think it's a  trust question it's more concern about  and what this is going to do to to 